  src/server.js          HTTP routes, wires tasks → notifications
  public/                index.html, app.js, styles.css

  Each file has one job and stays small enough to read in one sitting. [38;5;153mnotifications.js[39m is the only place that decides
  who hears about what.

  [1mSchema.[0m

  users          (id, name)                      [32m-- seeded fixtures; no auth[39m
  tasks          (id, title, description, status, assignee_id,
                  due_date, created_by, created_at, updated_at)
  subscriptions  (user_id, task_id, reason, created_at)   [32m-- PK (user_id, task_id)[39m
  notifications  (id, user_id, task_id, actor_id, type,
                  old_value, new_value, created_at, read_at)

  [38;5;153mreason[39m is [38;5;153mcreator | assignee | manual[39m — purely for explaining "why am I getting this?" in the UI; it doesn't affect
  delivery. Presence of the row [3mis[0m the subscription.

  [1mTwo decisions worth surfacing, because they're the ones people argue about later:[0m

  [3mSelf-actions don't notify you.[0m If you change the status yourself, you don't get a notification about it — but other
  subscribers do. Without this, every action you take pings you, and the feed becomes useless immediately.

  [3mUnfollow is a delete, not a tombstone.[0m If you unfollow a task, you stop hearing about it — until something
  re-subscribes you, e.g. someone assigns it to you again. That matches GitHub's behavior and keeps the table simple.
  The alternative is a [38;5;153mmuted[39m flag that survives re-assignment, which is a real feature but not one you asked for, and
  it's additive later.

  [1mDependencies: zero.[0m Node 22's built-in [38;5;153mnode:sqlite[39m and [38;5;153mnode:test[39m cover storage and testing, so there's no [38;5;153mnpm install[39m
  and no lockfile. If you're on an older Node, this becomes one dependency ([38;5;153mbetter-sqlite3[39m) — I'll check the version
  before writing the plan.

  Does the module split and schema look right?

